{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1444 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "618389aed37e734604d8e9e1e72d2a823f8431f06f04a288a96f782906772daa" :edb/db-before-t 1443 :edb/db-after-t 1444 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534756 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:57:44.312Z" 13194139534756 true] [17592186128838 1100 "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/159566da49ef22ea1aae7800355949ae3cf02dac6a6a02e0a8bc344db176a75d.png)" 13194139534756 true] [17592186128838 1100 "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/145658c34dbb00034b6a487c5e77e888.png)" 13194139534756 false] [17592186128838 1145 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=0$. Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(0) = -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = -2, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = 2\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=0$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3$, but they are removable discontinuities, *not* jump discontinuities." 13194139534756 false] [17592186128838 1145 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(0) = -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = -2, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = 2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=0$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ but they are removable discontinuities, *not* jump discontinuities." 13194139534756 true] [17592186128839 1100 "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/9e708e4fea2cd98e8d76978e58a4525d610d6f82521345b3aaa6232fd39b7539.png)" 13194139534756 true] [17592186128839 1100 "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/c6c40035c03ae2873bb952c1aba81138.png)" 13194139534756 false] [17592186128839 1145 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = -1, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = 1\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=1$, but this is not a jump discontinuity." 13194139534756 false] [17592186128839 1145 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = -1, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = 1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=1,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity." 13194139534756 true] [17592186128848 1100 "Does the function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/49e4cd87824a20b73175305aac7f3773f04a28e02cfa9c3c89cef9079a21ae8a.png) below have a jump discontinuity at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/22a0edba1f275de80719ea33fc4878640d2b2b93f8e5620c8999d6a449cdd35b.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/742066aa035c1b710ab30a74ff5320d3941aae17bbbb8a4c5edcf35ff67c5920.png)" 13194139534756 true] [17592186128848 1100 "Does the function $f(x)$ below have a jump discontinuity at $x=0$?\n\n${x^{2} + 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ 2x - 1,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0$" 13194139534756 false] [17592186128848 1145 "Let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/29d8d36954a25bff5808ce7c59dfc3ec0e373d22541cc2df85c93aa0a74c8bb4.png) exists ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/a4785336954b0736113ea8ad968aa60db31e55720ff5bdeb128597a8c7c72b90.png)\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/949d5bef0569fbd6f58382e07873412bb07cf3b735ae264c9cb78b366fa59bd2.png)\nwhile\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/d6dfbdbb30c840068ad7a86370f6e444453f925ba8869140b7288b51d164f228.png)\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/81510388d37345ec3bfb525da6795c62d4ef190798e1574139469b5a90d17d69.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/1d1ac18ca3b92f6df4647c49855e18d40ab7637269ff9b366edbfcaa96c4d59d.png) has a jump discontinuity at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/8f57be1d6e33e5ee1ad485bc58b29dbca4819ee229a505c0e7601150392d7615.png)" 13194139534756 true] [17592186128848 1145 "Let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)=1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to 0^-} 2x-1 = 2(0)-1 = -1$\nwhile\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to 0^+} x^2+1 = 0^2+1 = 1$.\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0$." 13194139534756 false] [17592186129022 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307836 13194139534756 true] [17592186129022 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307871 13194139534756 true] [17592186129022 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307912 13194139534756 true] [17592186129022 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307919 13194139534756 true] [17592186129023 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307843 13194139534756 true] [17592186129023 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307850 13194139534756 true] [17592186129023 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307898 13194139534756 true] [17592186129023 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307926 13194139534756 true] [17592186129032 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307857 13194139534756 true] [17592186129032 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307864 13194139534756 true] [17592186129032 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307878 13194139534756 true] [17592186129032 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307885 13194139534756 true] [17592186129032 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307905 13194139534756 true] [17592186129736 1017 #edb/ref 17592186128210 13194139534756 false] [17592186129736 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307892 13194139534756 true] [17592186129736 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534756 true] [17592186129736 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/f1e1a140e78362efddf48ba6744e2f88d109000fba8eae97818b024c2d249ad5.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139534756 true] [17592186129736 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/q-48119.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y = f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139534756 false] [17592186129736 1145 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity." 13194139534756 true] [17592186129739 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534756 true] [17592186129739 1100 "Which of the following functions has a jump discontinuity at $x =-1$?" 13194139534756 false] [17592186129739 1100 "Which of the following functions has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1?$" 13194139534756 true] [17592186129739 1145 "The function $y=f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/8961fba5e89b2c6e85879f448806b0cc348cf9d271e18606ad318c1b8bf9e13c.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities." 13194139534756 true] [17592186307836 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307837 13194139534756 true] [17592186307836 1089 "q-104577" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307836 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534756 true] [17592186307836 1100 "Which of the following functions has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3?$" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307836 1113 #uuid "d11a455c-5d50-5b38-8106-9406605cbcaa" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307836 1145 "The function $y=g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $g(-3)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)=-2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-captu