ave\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=4,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity." 13194139534756 true] [17592186307844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307845 13194139534756 true] [17592186307844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307846 13194139534756 true] [17592186307844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307847 13194139534756 true] [17592186307844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307848 13194139534756 true] [17592186307844 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307849 13194139534756 true] [17592186307844 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534756 true] [17592186307844 1041 "selection" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307844 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307845 13194139534756 true] [17592186307844 1110 #uuid "a2800d50-1de0-5a08-b6c5-2359fb2cd2ed" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307845 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=2$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307845 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307845 1111 #uuid "22cc68e7-a8d6-5d4a-a344-563e3fab6fdc" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307846 1034 "$x=2$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307846 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307846 1111 #uuid "b3adf4dd-1f02-5245-9046-dc54088264f4" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307847 1034 "$x=2$ and $x=4$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307847 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307847 1111 #uuid "392f495e-c19a-56f7-bbdf-0e99e267521d" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307848 1034 "$x=-1$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307848 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307848 1111 #uuid "a2140057-eb8c-5b52-a891-cd41551e9f2f" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307849 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=4$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307849 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307849 1111 #uuid "1946a391-5fa3-5df6-98c0-88fabb87a5ac" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307850 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307851 13194139534756 true] [17592186307850 1089 "q-104689" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307850 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534756 true] [17592186307850 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/2cb9e5fc9fb0e5b1ea95406ba9961cc176a9c637f354b1e8ff2d574cbb1261d6.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307850 1113 #uuid "a749e8c5-6c26-5b68-8d7b-2fba028032ee" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307850 1145 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a jump discontinuity.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=2$ only." 13194139534756 true] [17592186307851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307852 13194139534756 true] [17592186307851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307853 13194139534756 true] [17592186307851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307854 13194139534756 true] [17592186307851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307855 13194139534756 true] [17592186307851 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307856 13194139534756 true] [17592186307851 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534756 true] [17592186307851 1041 "selection" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307851 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307852 13194139534756 true] [17592186307851 1110 #uuid "baca7c6c-6162-5f87-b531-990dd4999cf5" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307852 1034 "$x=2$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307852 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307852 1111 #uuid "1b866054-3a56-5c16-9126-b3c1b3930f9d" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307853 1034 "$x=0$ and $x=2$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307853 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307853 1111 #uuid "d9e4be1c-f18d-59ec-ab82-2d44a3b0b569" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307854 1034 "$x=-2$ and $x=0$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307854 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307854 1111 #uuid "31d1a151-3e0a-58e2-94b1-23b0d6410ec8" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307855 1034 "$x=0$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307855 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307855 1111 #uuid "99a05726-39e5-5856-a4e4-d26c96192e94" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307856 1034 "$x=-2$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307856 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307856 1111 #uuid "143a7a97-7cf0-5875-b721-5f482e47caaa" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307857 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307858 13194139534756 true] [17592186307857 1089 "q-104693" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307857 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534756 true] [17592186307857 1100 "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=4?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5, & x<4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10, & x\\ge 4\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5-2x, & x\\le 4 \\\\ x+3, & x>4\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 5, & x=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307857 1113 #uuid "b7deaa4c-aeeb-5438-8da3-4314b016404f" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307857 1145 "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(4)={4}^{2}-10=6$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}({x}^{2}-10)={4}^{2}-10=6.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(4)=5-2(4)=-3$ exists  $\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}(5-2x)=5-2(4)=-3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(x+3)=4+3=7.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(4)=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5x=5(4)=20\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}5x=5(4)=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20\\ne h(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4.$" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307859 13194139534756 true] [17592186307858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307860 13194139534756 true] [17592186307858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307861 13194139534756 true] [17592186307858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307862 13194139534756 true] [17592186307858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307863 13194139534756 true] [17592186307858 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534756 true] [17592186307858 1041 "selection" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307858 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307859 13194139534756 true] [17592186307858 1110 #uuid "7ce5e39a-870a-599b-9544-4c2d817cf402" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307859 1034 "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307859 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307859 1111 #uuid "63f578e0-2ff8-593d-ad8e-6f335c8db098" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307860 1034 "$f(x)$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307860 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307860 1111 #uuid "0efa4ec4-a97a-5382-afc6-9bdc8a978f3a" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307861 1034 "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307861 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307861 1111 #uuid "ddc80786-fbd4-51a1-bd40-085ebb2b9029" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307862 1034 "$g(x)$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307862 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307862 1111 #uuid "47f0ee7b-0911-5509-b15e-be158a735d4b" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307863 1034 "$f(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307863 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307863 1111 #uuid "b1c0d324-463e-5107-9627-2e50ca558f70" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307864 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307865 13194139534756 true] [17592186307864 1089 "q-104701" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307864 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534756 true] [17592186307864 1100 "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=7?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2x-1, & x\\le 7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-30, & x>7,\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<7 \\\\ 5x+14, & x\\ge 7,\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-7}, & x<7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}, & x\\ge 7,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307864 1113 #uuid "24bfaf28-dbb7-577c-ab56-7ff13a09d906" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307864 1145 "For $f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(7)=2(7)-1=13$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}(2x-1)=2(7)-1=13\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}({x}^{2}-30)={7}^{2}-30=19.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=7.$\n\nNow, for $g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(7)=5(7)+14=49$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}{x}^{2}=49, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}(5x+14)=5(7)+14=49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)=49.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,7}g(x)=49=g(7).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$\n\nFor $h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(7)=\\frac{1}{7}$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x-7}=-\\infty \n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nSince the left limit is infinite, $h(x)$ does not have a jump discontinuity at $x=7.$" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307866 13194139534756 true] [17592186307865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307867 13194139534756 true] [17592186307865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307868 13194139534756 true] [17592186307865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307869 13194139534756 true] [17592186307865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307870 13194139534756 true] [17592186307865 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534756 true] [17592186307865 1041 "selection" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307865 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307867 13194139534756 true] [17592186307865 1110 #uuid "ab0198b4-3076-5f5c-9fba-8e21dc6f97a