54 13194139534756 true] [17592186307896 1111 #uuid "32ca1c72-6a60-59c6-9b87-1fdefa4b6c21" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307897 1034 "$x=-3$  only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307897 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307897 1111 #uuid "7b9c83c8-6024-50c0-ac7d-554313fab134" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307898 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307899 13194139534756 true] [17592186307898 1089 "q-48124" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307898 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534756 true] [17592186307898 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/83c19d6864e09383d64946b32d2ef9b98fc773c030f9922ad8843e54c60399d6.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307898 1113 #uuid "d48ac5a6-0498-5bfc-aced-fcaca9891cf6" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307898 1145 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to f(x)}$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-5$ and $x=-1,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=1$ only." 13194139534756 true] [17592186307899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307900 13194139534756 true] [17592186307899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307901 13194139534756 true] [17592186307899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307902 13194139534756 true] [17592186307899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307903 13194139534756 true] [17592186307899 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307904 13194139534756 true] [17592186307899 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534756 true] [17592186307899 1041 "selection" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307899 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307903 13194139534756 true] [17592186307899 1110 #uuid "9a697b6b-3770-50fb-a4fe-206ffb6140f4" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307900 1034 "$x=-5$ and $x=1$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307900 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307900 1111 #uuid "4ec29fc9-d967-5bd7-9a7e-99cf6038e527" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307901 1034 "$x=-5$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307901 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307901 1111 #uuid "0831cc40-029b-5e26-a001-abf6526242b4" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307902 1034 "$x=-5$ and $x=-1$" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307902 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307902 1111 #uuid "76d1bac2-4489-511e-bba3-eb3fc131a53e" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307903 1034 "$x=1$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307903 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307903 1111 #uuid "2a222f44-9bf3-57a5-a627-f3cdc3c21778" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307904 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=1$ only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307904 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307904 1111 #uuid "22e230bb-d2ef-5341-b366-f2db4ef4e1ce" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307905 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307906 13194139534756 true] [17592186307905 1089 "q-48167" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307905 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534756 true] [17592186307905 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3, & x<1 \\\\ 1-{x}^{3}, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$\n- It's continuous at $x=1.$" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307905 1113 #uuid "449cb233-0a42-5258-ab1c-39119094e289" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307905 1145 "For $x=1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1-{1}^{3}=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+3)=({1}^{2}+3)=4\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-{x}^{3})=(1-{1}^{3})=0.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nHence, I is true, while II and III are false." 13194139534756 true] [17592186307906 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307907 13194139534756 true] [17592186307906 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307908 13194139534756 true] [17592186307906 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307909 13194139534756 true] [17592186307906 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307910 13194139534756 true] [17592186307906 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307911 13194139534756 true] [17592186307906 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534756 true] [17592186307906 1041 "selection" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307906 1104 #edb/ref 17592186307908 13194139534756 true] [17592186307906 1110 #uuid "103287d0-37e3-5298-97d8-6af14a575728" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307907 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307907 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307907 1111 #uuid "9f4e3389-fc6b-50ca-8bf3-6a31009db2ca" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307908 1034 "I only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307908 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307908 1111 #uuid "db7df812-7d28-53f8-bfb4-16c2272ff7e7" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307909 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307909 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307909 1111 #uuid "da430fae-ce08-5b61-9ef6-bbad81747348" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307910 1034 "II only" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307910 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [17592186307910 1111 #uuid "de1cc7da-6d7a-5770-9d82-5159bebc56a9" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307911 1034 "I and III" 13194139534756 true] [17592186307911 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534756 true] [1759